Building MoCoLinux on a current Linux distribution
===============================================================================

doc/building is the original 2011 guide and still describes the intended design
(configure, bin/*.sh, the comake system). This file records what actually works
on a current host, and why the differences exist.

Verified on CachyOS, kernel 7.1.5, on 2026-07-30:

    Python      3.14.6      comake is ported to Python 3
    GCC         16.1.1      host compiler
    mingw-w64   16.1.0      i686-w64-mingw32- and x86_64-w64-mingw32-
    binutils    2.46        mingw-w64 targets

Result: linux.sys plus colinux-daemon, colinux-net-daemon,
colinux-slirp-net-daemon, colinux-ndis-net-daemon, colinux-serial-daemon,
colinux-debug-daemon and colinux-console-nt, from a clean tree, with no errors
and no warning suppressions.


1. Prerequisites
----------------

    pacman -S mingw-w64-gcc mingw-w64-binutils mingw-w64-crt mingw-w64-headers

quilt is optional; bin/build-kernel.sh falls back to plain patch(1) without it.


2. Guest kernel source
----------------------

The host driver includes the passage-page ABI header out of the guest kernel
tree, so a patched tree is needed even to build the Windows side.

    mkdir -p ../download ../build
    curl -L -C - -o ../download/linux-2.6.33.7.tar.bz2 \
      https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v2.6/linux-2.6.33.7.tar.bz2
    bzip2 -t ../download/linux-2.6.33.7.tar.bz2

Check the size: 66240255 bytes. A truncated download can still exit 0.

    tar xf ../download/linux-2.6.33.7.tar.bz2 -C ../build
    mv ../build/linux-2.6.33.7 ../build/linux-2.6.33.7-source
    cd ../build/linux-2.6.33.7-source
    grep -v '^#' <path-to-tree>/patch/series-2.6.33.7 | while read name level; do
        patch ${level:--p1} -f -s --no-backup-if-mismatch \
            < <path-to-tree>/patch/$name || echo "FAILED: $name"
    done

All 29 patches apply cleanly. Confirm the shared ABI header landed:

    ls arch/x86/include/asm/cooperative.h include/linux/cooperative.h


3. Building
-----------

./configure refuses to write bin/user-build.cfg unless it finds a guest
compiler whose version matches the host compiler's (see section 6), so drive
comake directly:

    cd src
    export COLINUX_ARCH=i386
    export COLINUX_HOST_OS=winnt
    export COLINUX_TARGET_KERNEL_SOURCE=$PWD/../../build/linux-2.6.33.7-source
    export COLINUX_TARGET_KERNEL_BUILD=$PWD/../../build/linux-2.6.33.7-build
    python3 ../bin/make.py colinux

COLINUX_TARGET_KERNEL_BUILD does not have to exist. Set it to something other
than the source directory: it selects the out-of-tree include list. (Both lists
now carry arch/x86/include, so in-source builds work too, but out-of-tree is
what has actually been exercised.)

Output lands in src/colinux/os/winnt/build/. `python3 ../bin/make.py clean`
removes it.

Environment variables that matter:

    COLINUX_ARCH                i386 (default) or, in future, x86_64. Selects
                                src/colinux/arch/<arch> via the arch/current
                                symlink.
    COLINUX_HOST_OS             winnt (default) or linux.
    COLINUX_HOST_CROSS_PREFIX   Override the cross prefix. Defaults to
                                i686-w64-mingw32- for i386 and
                                x86_64-w64-mingw32- for x86_64.
    COLINUX_DDK_INCLUDE         Override the DDK include directory. Normally
                                found by asking the compiler.
    COLINUX_CFLAGS              Extra compiler flags.
    COLINUX_ENABLE_FLTK=yes     Build colinux-console-fltk.exe. Needs a
                                hand-built patched FLTK 1.1.10 for mingw.
    COLINUX_ENABLE_WX=yes       Build colinux-console-wx.exe. Needs wxWidgets.
    COLINUX_ENABLE_WINPCAP=yes  Build colinux-bridged-net-daemon.exe. Needs the
                                WinPcap developer pack for pcap.h.

Sanity check on the driver:

    i686-w64-mingw32-objdump -p linux.sys | grep 'DLL Name'

It should import ntoskrnl.exe, HAL.dll and NDIS.SYS, and nothing else.


4. The broken ndis.h, and how it is worked around
-------------------------------------------------

The whole driver builds, conet.c included, so linux.sys carries the NDIS protocol
binding behind the "ndis-bridge" network type and imports NDIS.SYS. Getting there
needs a workaround, because as of mingw-w64 16.1 ddk/ndis.h does not compile:

    NdisMWanIndicateReceiveComplete(
            IN NDIS_HANDLE  MiniportAdapterHandle     <- missing comma
            IN NDIS_HANDLE  NdisLinkContext);

The declaration is in the body of the header, not behind an optional guard, so
no NDIS driver can include ndis.h at all. This is an upstream bug and is worth
reporting.

make_ndis_compat_include() in src/build.comake.py derives a corrected copy from
whatever ndis.h is installed, writes it to
src/colinux/os/winnt/kernel/ndis-compat/ddk/ndis.h, and prepends that directory
to the driver's include path. It reports what it did:

    Repaired 1 missing comma(s) in a local copy of .../ddk/ndis.h

Deriving the copy at build time rather than vendoring a snapshot of a 12,000-line
header means it tracks whatever the distribution ships. If the bug is fixed
upstream, zero commas are inserted, nothing is generated, and the system header
is used directly -- so the workaround retires itself.

The generated directory is gitignored. `make clean` does not remove it; delete
src/colinux/os/winnt/kernel/ndis-compat by hand if you want to force it to be
regenerated.

conet.c also used to carry local reimplementations of NdisInitializeEvent,
NdisWaitEvent, NdisSetEvent and NdisResetEvent, added because the w32api NDIS
headers of the time did not declare them. mingw-w64 declares all four and libndis
exports them, so those were removed in favour of the real ones. Both
NdisWaitEvent call sites pass MsToWait = 0, which the real function treats as an
indefinite wait, matching the behaviour of the code that was removed.


5. What is genuinely not built
------------------------------

colinux-console-fltk.exe, colinux-console-wx.exe and
colinux-bridged-net-daemon.exe each need a third-party library that is not
present and has to be built by hand: FLTK 1.1.10, wxWidgets, and the WinPcap
developer pack respectively. All three are opt-in, as the wx console already was
upstream. No coLinux code was changed for this and nothing is stubbed -- supply
the library, set the flag, and they build. Upstream's `make download` fetches
and builds them; wiring that back up is unfinished work, not a decision.

Notes on how much each is actually worth:

FLTK is the one that matters, because daemon.c defaults start_parameters->console
to "fltk", so a stock `colinux-daemon @foo.conf` fails at "error launching
console" until you pass -t nt or -d. It is also the only console with scrollback,
font selection and copy/paste, all added in 0.7.8. The dependency is lighter than
it looks: patch/fltk-1.1.10-win32.diff is 799 bytes and does two unremarkable
things, namely let FLTK's configure skip its X11 requirement when X is disabled,
and #define NO_TRACK_MOUSE to avoid an undefined __TrackMouseEvent@4. It is stock
FLTK plus two workarounds, not a fork. The fl_x_global_event_hook API hook that
./configure looks for belongs to patch/fltk-1.1.10-linux-patch.diff and only
matters for Linux as host. The open question is just whether 2009-vintage C++
still compiles under GCC 16.

wxWidgets buys nothing. The wx console was already opt-in upstream, so it was
never part of the shipped product, and it is a third console doing what the FLTK
and NT consoles already do.

WinPcap serves exactly one of the six network types in
src/colinux/common/config.h: pcap-bridge. The other five -- TAP, slirp,
ndis-bridge, ndis-nat, ndis-host -- all build and work today. WinPcap itself has
been discontinued since 2013 and superseded by Npcap, and ndis-bridge does host
bridging from inside the driver without needing any third-party packet driver
installed. If pcap bridging is ever wanted, target Npcap rather than reviving a
2007 SDK.

For development, colinux-console-nt.exe needs nothing external. Better still for
early boot, the guest config has CONFIG_SERIAL_COOPERATIVE_CONSOLE=y and
colinux-serial-daemon.exe builds, so the boot console can be taken over a virtual
serial line into a file on the host -- which works before the console driver is
up, and is the right tool for bring-up.

The tap-win32 driver is a separate driver and is not part of this build. It uses
the Rtl string helpers from ddk/ntstrsafe.h and will need that include added
locally when it is built, since ddk.h deliberately does not provide it.


6. The compiler-version check, and the guest kernel
---------------------------------------------------

A built vmlinux is not needed to build the Windows side, and none is built here.
Two things block it, both worth understanding before starting:

GCC 16 cannot compile Linux 2.6.33. A period-appropriate compiler is needed,
either built through configure --gcc-guest-build or supplied by a container with
an era-appropriate distribution.

More awkwardly, colinux-daemon refuses to start a kernel whose __GXX_ABI_VERSION
differs from its own (src/colinux/user/daemon.c, CO_RC(COMPILER_MISMATCHED)).
Modern GCC reports 1021; GCC 4.1 reports 1002. So a guest kernel built with an
old compiler will be rejected by daemons built with a new one, and the two
toolchains cannot simply be mixed.

Note the check only compares __GXX_ABI_VERSION, a C++ ABI marker, as a proxy for
"same compiler". The declared major/minor versions are printed but not compared.
It is a crude test for what is really a plain-C struct layout question, and it
becomes largely unnecessary once the shared structs use explicit fixed-width
types -- which is milestone M1 in doc/porting-x86_64. Relaxing it is the
intended way out, not building an entire 2011 toolchain.


7. Remaining warnings
---------------------

The build is clean but not silent. By volume:

  121  stack probing requires '-maccumulate-outgoing-args' for correctness
   76  old-style function definition (slirp, K&R definitions)
   23  array subscript 0 is outside the bounds of an interior zero-length array
   20  Please include winsock2.h before windows.h
   18  each of several errno macros redefined (slirp against mingw's errno.h)

The first deserves attention rather than suppression. coLinux passes
-mno-accumulate-outgoing-args on i386 specifically to pin the stack-argument ABI
that the passage assembly reads at fixed %esp offsets, and GCC now says that
interacts badly with stack probing. Nothing undefined appears at link time, so
no probe helper is actually being emitted, but this is worth settling before
trusting the i386 passage code -- and it is a reason the x86-64 port should not
inherit that flag.

The zero-length-array warnings are the flip side of the flexible-array-member
change described in the commit "Build the WinNT driver and daemons with a current
toolchain": GCC knows params[0] and extra_data[0] have no elements and objects
to indexing them. The indexing is intentional and the arrays run into the space
that follows.
